  "description": "Pushup Tracker got its name because the app started with push-ups. Now the camera counts squats and pull-ups too, in half of the ten training plans push-ups are just one exercise among many, and the exercise wiki has 40 entries. Why the name no longer does it justice, what changes — and what definitely stays the same.",
  "publishedAt": "2026-09-20",
  "content": "<h2>The Name Promises Less Than the App Can Do</h2>\n<p>\n  When this app was created, it counted push-ups. Nothing else. The name was back then a description, not a brand: <em>Pushup Tracker</em>, because it said exactly what the thing did.\n</p>\n<p>\n  That's no longer true. If you turn on the camera today, you can also count squats, pull-ups, and sit-ups — and for plank and hollow hold, a hold timer runs instead of a counter. The exercise wiki has 40 entries, from lunges to deadlift patterns to mobility. The catalog from which goals, analysis, and plans draw contains 42 exercises in nine categories: push-ups, pressing, pulling, squats, hip hinges, lunges, core, cardio, and mobility.\n</p>\n<p>\n  It becomes clearest with the training plans. There are ten — and in half of them, the push-up is just one exercise among many, not the goal:\n</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Push &amp; Pull Balance</strong> — six weeks to balance pressing and pulling</li>\n  <li><strong>Full Body Strong</strong> — six weeks of squats, hip hinges, lunges, core, and mobility</li>\n  <li><strong>Core Foundations</strong> — four weeks of core work</li>\n  <li><strong>HIIT Burner</strong> — four weeks of conditioning</li>\n  <li><strong>Mobility &amp; Recovery</strong> — two weeks of deload</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\n  Push-ups certainly appear in them — but as a building block, not as the goal. Full Body Strong, for example, prescribes 84 exercise units over six weeks, 24 of which are push-ups; the remaining 60 are core, hip hinges, squats, lunges, and mobility.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Add to that freely customizable sessions, friends list and leaderboard, badges, streaks, heatmap, and daily goals — all in nine languages. None of that is in the name.\n</p><h2>Why This Is More Than Cosmetics</h2>\n<p>\n  A name that promises too little is not a harmless cosmetic flaw. It filters people out before they even see the app.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Someone looking for a squat tracker won't click on something called \"Pushup.\" Someone wanting to track their full-body program assumes they're in the wrong place. And someone coming in through one of our articles on pull-ups, core training, or Zone 2 cardio lands on an app whose name contradicts what they just read.\n</p>\n<p>\n  That's the real reason for the change: not that we don't like the name anymore, but that it keeps people from finding something that would help them.\n</p><h2>What's Guaranteed to Stay</h2>\n<p>\n  First, the most important thing: <strong>push-ups are not going away.</strong> They were the beginning, they are the most developed part of the app, and they will stay that way. The push-up wiki with its 13 variants remains complete. The five push-up plans stay. The camera counter for push-ups remains what it is — the feature that started everything.\n</p>\n<p>\n  What changes is the umbrella brand, not the product. Push-ups stop being the name above everything and become what they already are: <em>a first-rate exercise among several.</em>\n</p>\n<p>\n  And: <strong>your account, your entries, your streak, and your badges remain untouched.</strong> A rename is not a restart. There is nothing to export and nothing to set up again.\n</p><h2>What You Need to Do Now</h2>\n<p>\n  Nothing. The new name isn't final yet, and we'll introduce it only when it is — along with everything that comes with it. Should anything change about addresses or links later, we'll announce it in advance and make sure old links keep working.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Until then, nothing changes for you whatsoever. Keep training, keep your streak — and don't be surprised when a different name appears above the page soon.\n</p><aside class=\"plan-cta\">\n  <h3>Full Body Strong – 6-Week Full-Body Plan</h3>\n  <p>\n    If you want to understand what \"more than push-ups\" means: this plan combines push-ups with squats, hip hinges, lunges, core, and mobility — three sessions per week, no equipment needed.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <a href=\"/training-plans/full-body-6w\">Start the full-body plan &rarr;</a>\n  </p>\n</aside>",
